          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="6.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Steam Cleaner Types</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#psi-temp" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. PSI and Temperature</a>
          <a href="#refill" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Refill vs Continuous</a>
          <a href="#by-job" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Pick by Job</a>
          <a href="#sanitize" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. What Steam Can't Sanitize</a>
          <a href="#surfaces" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Surfaces Never to Steam</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Safety and Recalls</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Water and Maintenance</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Short answer: if you want to clean grout, kitchens and upholstery, buy a pressurized canister, and the Vapamore MR-100 Primo is the one we would pick for its roughly 50 PSI rating and lifetime boiler warranty. The McCulloch MC1275 is the budget canister. If you only clean sealed floors, a steam mop like the Bissell PowerFresh is faster and cheaper. Do not expect any of them to replace a registered disinfectant.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run these steam cleaners in a lab or a test kitchen, and we will not pretend we did. This guide is built from the documents that actually describe what a steamer can do: maker spec sheets, owner's manuals, warranty terms, retailer spec tables, the Consumer Product Safety Commission recall database, and the patterns that show up again and again in owner reports. Where a figure comes from a single retailer listing rather than the maker, we say so. Where makers disagree with themselves across listings, we use the more conservative number.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first filter was category. A steam mop, a handheld steamer and a canister steam cleaner are three different machines that happen to share a word. A steam mop heats a small amount of water on demand and pushes low-pressure vapor into a cloth pad. A handheld is a small pressurized kettle with a trigger. A canister is a real boiler, usually 40 to 60 ounces, that builds pressure before you start and holds it for most of an hour. We picked at least one model in each class so that the right answer exists for every job, instead of ranking a $60 mop against a $300 boiler and calling one of them the winner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inside each class we weighted four things. Rated pressure and boiler size come first on canisters, because those two numbers decide whether a steamer can lift grout grime or only warm it. Run time per fill and whether the machine can be refilled while hot come next, since they decide how much of a bathroom you finish before you stop and wait. Warranty length on the boiler matters more than on almost any other appliance, because a corroded or scaled boiler is how these machines die. Finally we looked at the recall record and at what owners commonly report going wrong after a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also left things out on purpose. Several well-reviewed steamers were dropped because their published specs were vague, inconsistent across listings, or missing a pressure rating entirely. A steamer that will not tell you its PSI is usually a steamer with a low one. The Dupray Neat, long one of the most recommended canisters online, is covered in the safety section rather than in our picks, because of a 2026 boiler-rupture recall that every buyer of a used or older unit needs to know about.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Canister vs Handheld vs Steam Mop vs 2-in-1
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most disappointment with steam cleaners comes from buying the wrong type, not a bad model. Someone buys a steam mop expecting it to strip black grout lines, or buys a canister to freshen a kitchen floor every other day and finds it takes ten minutes to heat up. The table below is the short version. The paragraphs after it explain where each type earns its money and where it does not.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">How it makes steam</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical pressure</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best at</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Weak at</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Canister</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sealed boiler, heats then holds pressure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40 to 75 PSI</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grout, tile, ovens, upholstery, car interiors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Quick daily floor jobs, storage space</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Handheld</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small pressurized kettle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually unpublished or low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sinks, faucets, one-off spots</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Anything bigger than a vanity</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Steam mop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water drips onto a heating plate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not pressurized</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sealed tile and sealed vinyl floors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grout lines, deep grime, laminate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2-in-1 mop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mop with a detachable steam pod</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not pressurized</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Floors plus light above-floor work</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hard scrubbing jobs</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A canister is the only type that genuinely cleans grout, because pressure is what drives steam into the porous joint and blows loosened grime out of it. The trade-off is setup. Every canister here needs 8 to 12 minutes to come up to pressure, and the classic designs cannot be opened until they cool and depressurize. If your main targets are a shower surround, a stove top, a greasy range hood filter or the seats of a car, this is the category to shop.
          </p>

          <p>
            A handheld steamer is a canister with most of the boiler removed. It is cheap, it is fast, and it runs out of water in roughly ten minutes. It is excellent at things like the base of a faucet, the track of a sliding shower door or a sticky spot on a counter. It is not a whole-bathroom tool, and owners who buy one for that job commonly report spending more time refilling and reheating than cleaning.
          </p>

          <p>
            A steam mop is a floor tool. It is the fastest way to sanitize-level clean a sealed tile or sealed vinyl floor without a bucket, and it is ready in under a minute. It is not a grout cleaner, despite what the box art implies. Because the steam passes through a cloth pad at low pressure, it mostly wipes the tile face and glides over the joints. Think of it as a better wet mop, and pair it with a canister or a stiff brush for the lines between tiles. If you want a bucket-based option for floors that should not see steam at all, our guide to the <a href="/best-spin-mop/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best spin mops</a> covers that side.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 2-in-1 mop adds a removable pod you can carry to a counter or a shower door. It is a sensible compromise for an apartment where storage space matters more than raw power. Just understand that the pod has a mop's steam output, not a canister's, so it handles light above-floor work rather than replacing a real boiler.
          </p>
        </div>

