        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy the steamer that matches the job. A pressurized canister like the Vapamore MR-100 or McCulloch MC1275 is the only type that properly cleans grout, kitchens and upholstery. A steam mop is a fast floor tool for sealed tile, and a handheld is a spot cleaner. Continuous-fill machines like the Reliable Brio Plus only make sense if you steam for hours.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you buy, use distilled water, fill only to the line, keep it off laminate and unsealed wood, and treat sanitizing claims with skepticism. Steam is a superb cleaner and a mediocre disinfectant. Used for what it does well, it replaces a cabinet full of sprays.
          </p>
